   "book": "Ce este aceasta?",
   "spoken": "Ce-i asta?",
   "gloss": "What's this?",
   "note": "For objects this is the everyday form: este contracts to -i, and aceasta shortens to asta. You will hear 'Ce-i asta?' far more than the textbook's full 'Ce este aceasta?'. Fully neutral for things. The book itself shows the halfway step, 'Acesta-i un stilou', so the contraction is already in the source.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "type": "register_scale",
   "spoken": "Cine e? / Cine-i ăsta?",
   "gloss": "Who is it? / Who's this (guy)?",
   "note": "The same asta/ăsta shortening is fine for objects but turns familiar and can sound dismissive about a person ('who's this guy?'). With someone you have just met, keep 'Cine e?' or name the person. Save ăsta/asta-for-people for friends.",
   "confidence": "check"
  },
  {
   "type": "missing_from_book",
   "spoken": "Cum se spune ... în română?",
   "gloss": "How do you say ... in Romanian?",
   "note": "The chapter teaches you to ask 'what is this?' when the thing is in front of you, but never how to ask for a word you don't know. This is the phrase that actually unblocks you in a class. Grammar beyond this lesson; take it whole.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "type": "missing_from_book",
   "spoken": "Ce înseamnă ...?",
   "gloss": "What does ... mean?",
